.TH ATLAS-CURL 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-curl \- Send exactly the HTTP request you describe and show exactly what comes back.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
curl \-s \-i \-X POST \-H 'X\-Role: admin' \-b 'session=abc' \-d 'user=alice' http://127.0.0.1:8099/login
 |    |  |  |       |                 |                 |                |             '\-\-\- URL
 |    |  |  |       |                 |                 |                '\-\-\- request body
 |    |  |  |       |                 |                 '\-\-\- cookie header
 |    |  |  |       |                 '\-\-\- add or replace a header
 |    |  |  |       '\-\-\- method (curl picks POST for \-d by itself)
 |    |  |  '\-\-\- \-i = show response headers too
 |    |  '\-\-\- \-s = silent: no progress bar
 '\-\-\- the program
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Test a web challenge without a browser: change a header, cookie, method or body and see the response.
.IP \(bu 2
Download a file, follow a redirect, check a status code in a script.
.IP \(bu 2
Reproduce what Burp or the browser sent, as a copy\-paste command.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-s / \-S\fR
Silent / show errors even when silent.
.br
Use it when: Scripts and clean output.
.TP
\fB\-i / \-I\fR
Include response headers / HEAD request only.
.br
Use it when: See status line, cookies, redirects.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Verbose: show the request and response headers.
.br
Use it when: See exactly what is sent.
.TP
\fB\-X METHOD\fR
Set the method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE).
.br
Use it when: Test method handling.
.TP
\fB\-H 'Name: value'\fR
Add or replace a header.
.br
Use it when: User\-Agent, Referer, Authorization, X\-Forwarded\-For.
.TP
\fB\-A / \-e\fR
User\-Agent / Referer shortcuts.
.br
Use it when: Natas\-style Referer checks.
.TP
\fB\-b 'k=v' / \-c file\fR
Send cookies / save cookies.
.br
Use it when: Session and cookie tampering.
.TP
\fB\-d / \-\-data\-urlencode\fR
Request body (form data) / URL\-encode a value.
.br
Use it when: Forms and logins.
.TP
\fB\-\-json '{...}'\fR
Send JSON with the right headers (recent curl).
.br
Use it when: APIs.
.TP
\fB\-u user:pass\fR
HTTP Basic authentication.
.br
Use it when: Basic\-auth pages (note: it is only base64).
.TP
\fB\-L\fR
Follow redirects.
.br
Use it when: Reach the final page.
.TP
\fB\-o FILE / \-O\fR
Save the body to a file / use the remote name.
.br
Use it when: Download.
.TP
\fB\-w '%{http_code}'\fR
Print a field after the transfer.
.br
Use it when: Status codes in loops.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR
Ignore TLS certificate errors.
.br
Use it when: Lab self\-signed certificates only.
.TP
\fB\-\-path\-as\-is\fR
Do not squash \fB/../\fR in the URL.
.br
Use it when: Path traversal tests.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-time N\fR
Give up after N seconds.
.br
Use it when: Scripts.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. A plain GET"
This lab server echoes back what it received, so you can see the request as the server saw it.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-s 'http://127.0.0.1:8099/hello?x=1'
.fi
.PP
.nf
GET /hello?x=1
User\-Agent: curl/8.5.0
Accept: */*
.fi
.PP
The first line is method and path; the rest are headers curl sent by default (\fBUser\-Agent\fR, \fBAccept\fR).
.SS "2. See the response headers"
\fB\-i\fR includes the status line and headers. \fB\-s\fR hides the progress meter.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-si http://127.0.0.1:8099/ | head \-8
.fi
.PP
.nf
HTTP/1.0 200 OK
Server: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.12.3
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:22:31 GMT
Content\-Type: text/plain
X\-Demo: echo

GET /
User\-Agent: curl/8.5.0
.fi
.PP
Status \fB200 OK\fR, a \fBContent\-Type\fR, and the custom \fBX\-Demo\fR header. Always read headers in web challenges: cookies and hints live there.
.SS "3. Change headers and send a cookie"
Headers and cookies are client\-controlled; a server must never trust them for access control.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-s \-H 'X\-Role: admin' \-A 'MyAgent/1.0' \-e 'http://natas5.example' \-b 'loggedin=1' http://127.0.0.1:8099/
.fi
.PP
.nf
GET /
User\-Agent: MyAgent/1.0
Accept: */*
Referer: http://natas5.example
Cookie: loggedin=1
X\-Role: admin
.fi
.PP
The echo shows each of your values arriving: User\-Agent, Referer, Cookie and X\-Role.
.SS "4. POST a form and JSON"
\fB\-d\fR switches to POST and sets a form content type; for JSON you set the header yourself.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-s \-d 'user=alice&pass=1' http://127.0.0.1:8099/login; echo; echo \-\-\-\-; curl \-s \-H 'Content\-Type: application/json' \-d '{"a":1}' http://127.0.0.1:8099/api
.fi
.PP
.nf
POST /login
User\-Agent: curl/8.5.0
Accept: */*
Content\-Length: 17
Content\-Type: application/x\-www\-form\-urlencoded

user=alice&pass=1
\-\-\-\-
POST /api
User\-Agent: curl/8.5.0
Accept: */*
Content\-Type: application/json
Content\-Length: 7

{"a":1}
.fi
.PP
The body appears after a blank line, with different \fBContent\-Type\fR values for the two requests.
.SS "5. Basic auth is just base64"
\fB\-u\fR sends \fBAuthorization: Basic <base64(user:pass)>\fR. Anyone who sees the request can decode it.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-s \-u alice:secret http://127.0.0.1:8099/ | grep Authorization; echo 'YWxpY2U6c2VjcmV0' | base64 \-d; echo
.fi
.PP
.nf
Authorization: Basic YWxpY2U6c2VjcmV0
alice:secret
.fi
.PP
The header carries the encoded credentials and \fBbase64 \-d\fR recovers them. This is why Basic auth needs HTTPS.
.SS "6. Redirects"
Without \fB\-L\fR you see the 301 and its \fBLocation\fR header; with \fB\-L\fR curl follows to the final page. \fB\-w\fR prints a summary.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-si http://127.0.0.1:8099/old | head \-3; echo \-\-\-; curl \-sL \-o /dev/null \-w 'final=%{url_effective} code=%{http_code}\en' http://127.0.0.1:8099/old
.fi
.PP
.nf
HTTP/1.0 301 Moved Permanently
Server: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.12.3
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:22:31 GMT
\-\-\-
final=http://127.0.0.1:8099/new code=200
.fi
.PP
Seeing the redirect response itself matters: sometimes the useful content is in the redirect, not the destination (Natas 22).
.SS "7. Status code only, for scripting"
\fB\-o /dev/null\fR discards the body; \fB\-w\fR prints the status. Loop over paths to build a tiny scanner.
.PP
.nf
$ for p in / /admin /missing; do curl \-s \-o /dev/null \-w "$p %{http_code}\en" http://127.0.0.1:8099$p; done
.fi
.PP
.nf
/ 200
/admin 403
/missing 404
.fi
.PP
200 for the home page, 403 (forbidden: it exists but you may not read it) for \fB/admin\fR and 404 (not found) for \fB/missing\fR. The difference between 403 and 404 tells you what exists. A real scanner (ffuf, gobuster) does this faster.
.SS "8. Show the raw request curl sends"
\fB\-v\fR prints lines starting with \fB>\fR (request) and \fB<\fR (response). Redirecting stdout hides the body.
.PP
.nf
$ curl \-sv http://127.0.0.1:8099/ 2>&1 >/dev/null | grep \-E '^(>|<)'
.fi
.PP
.nf
> GET / HTTP/1.1
> Host: 127.0.0.1:8099
> User\-Agent: curl/8.5.0
> Accept: */*
> 
< HTTP/1.0 200 OK
< Server: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.12.3
< Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:22:31 GMT
< Content\-Type: text/plain
< X\-Demo: echo
< 
.fi
.PP
This is the exact text on the wire. If a challenge says the server checks a header, this is where you confirm it was sent.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Quote URLs that contain \fB&\fR or \fB?\fR: the shell treats \fB&\fR as 'run in background'.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-d\fR implies POST and \fBapplication/x\-www\-form\-urlencoded\fR; use \fB\-G\fR to put data in the URL instead.
.IP \(bu 2
Use \fB\-\-data\-urlencode\fR for values with spaces or symbols, or they are sent raw.
.IP \(bu 2
curl may normalise \fB/../\fR in the URL; add \fB\-\-path\-as\-is\fR to send it unchanged.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-k\fR disables certificate checks: fine for your own lab, never for real credentials.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Send the request that makes the lab echo header \fBX\-Forwarded\-For: 127.0.0.1\fR.
.IP 2. 3
Write a loop that prints the status for 10 paths from a list in a file.
.IP 3. 3
Reproduce a browser request from DevTools: right\-click, Copy as cURL.
.SH SEE ALSO
wget, httpie, Burp Repeater, ffuf, nc (raw HTTP), python requests.
